# The ports of the editor.
#
# The project has a port of its own for the local work, so the editor does not collide with the other
# projects of the machine. Both values are read through dotenv, by the vite config and by the Playwright
# setup, and the defaults written in those configs are the same numbers: a clone without a `.env` works.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` to change them. `.env` is not committed, this file is its documentation.
#
# The numbers are chosen to be free on Linux, macOS and Windows at the same time:
#
# - they are below the ephemeral range of every one of them (Linux 32768-60999, macOS and Windows
#   49152-65535), so the system never hands a port out to an outgoing connection and takes it away
# - no default service of those systems listens on them (macOS AirPlay is 5000 and 7000, Windows has its
#   RPC, SMB and RDP ports, Linux has none here)
# - they are not on the blocked ports of the browsers: firefox answers a request to some of them with
#   "this address is restricted" (6000 is one, hence the pair here), and the editor has to open in firefox
# - 5555 is left out on purpose: android wireless debugging (`adb tcpip 5555`) uses it

# The port the development server (`npm run dev`) serves the editor on: http://127.0.0.1:5556/editor/
# The server runs with `strictPort`, so a taken port fails instead of silently moving to another one.
DEV_PORT=5556

# The port the browser tests (`npm run test:e2e`) start their own development server on. It is a separate
# server on purpose: a hot reload of the developer's server must not change the page under a test.
E2E_PORT=5557
